<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#cloves">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cloves</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cloves are the aromatic dried flower buds of a tree native to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Maluku Islands</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. They were among the most valuable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> of the Asian trade. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> competed for control of their production, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> enforcing a strict monopoly.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cloves.jpg" alt="Cloves" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Eugen Köhler, Köhler's Medizinal-Pflanzen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Syzygium_aromaticum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-030.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The spice monopolies enforced across the Maluku Islands, covering nutmeg, <b>cloves</b> and mace, were maintained through deliberate violence, including the Dutch conquest of the Banda Islands under Jan Pieterszoon Coen in 1621, with the near-extermination and enslavement of their population.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its function was collection and redistribution: <b>cloves</b>, nutmeg and mace from the Maluku Islands; cotton textiles from Gujarat, the Coromandel and Malabar coasts and Bengal; porcelain, silk and iron from China; camphor, sandalwood, spices, fish roe and seaweed from the archipelago.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The spice trade was enormously profitable in the decades around 1500–1550: pepper, cinnamon, <b>cloves</b> and nutmeg generated revenues that made Lisbon one of Europe’s wealthiest cities and funded the Manueline building programme, including the Jerónimos Monastery and the Tower of Belém.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Sayyid Said bin Sultan, ruler of Oman from 1806, turned that claim into an empire: he subdued the rival Mazrui governors of Mombasa by 1837, encouraged the planting of <b>cloves</b> on Zanzibar and Pemba, and from about 1840 made Zanzibar town his principal residence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Most people have lived by farming maize, cassava, rice and bananas, while exports have included coffee, cotton, sisal, cashew nuts, tea and Zanzibar’s <b>cloves</b>, and later gold, tourism and natural gas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tanzania">Tanzania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 19th century the sultans of Oman moved their capital to Zanzibar, in 1840, and the island became the centre of a commercial empire built on <b>cloves</b>, ivory and the slave trade.</blockquote><a href="/language/swahili">Swahili · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
